WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.218 --> 00:00:01.960
♪ [intro music] ♪

00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:07.221
Give me one minute,

00:00:07.221 --> 00:00:08.622
and I'll show you how Workflows

00:00:08.622 --> 00:00:11.340
can orchestrate and automate
your API-driven workflows.

00:00:11.585 --> 00:00:13.349
Workflows is a fully managed service

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:17.104
that gives you the power to integrate
tasks, microservices, and APIs.

00:00:17.456 --> 00:00:20.143
Reliable execution lets you create
dependable workflows

00:00:20.143 --> 00:00:21.981
that can automate complex processes

00:00:21.981 --> 00:00:25.895
across any Google Cloud products
or HTTP-based APIs.

00:00:25.895 --> 00:00:27.226
Since it's fully serverless,

00:00:27.226 --> 00:00:28.655
you can focus on the logic

00:00:28.655 --> 00:00:31.285
and not worry about the infrastructure 
or how to scale.

00:00:31.601 --> 00:00:33.786
Built-in error handling and retry policies

00:00:33.786 --> 00:00:36.519
give you a highly reliable
and resilient architecture.

00:00:36.926 --> 00:00:40.592
Dashboards give you true visibility
into what's happening and when.

00:00:41.528 --> 00:00:42.703
Getting started is easy.

00:00:43.223 --> 00:00:45.228
Using the console or the command line,

00:00:45.228 --> 00:00:48.538
you can define your workflow
as a simple YAML or JSON file

00:00:48.538 --> 00:00:51.498
to spell out which services
and APIs you want to include.

00:00:52.245 --> 00:00:54.849
Each service can also pass
information to the next,

00:00:55.197 --> 00:00:56.821
letting you build custom workflows

00:00:56.821 --> 00:00:58.753
that match the business logic you need.

00:00:59.115 --> 00:01:00.571
And since it's all serverless,

00:01:00.571 --> 00:01:02.249
you only pay for what you need,

00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:04.269
scaling down to zero during slow times.

00:01:04.903 --> 00:01:06.609
Let's look at an example use case.

00:01:07.142 --> 00:01:09.141
Maybe you have a website
called BeyondTree,

00:01:09.141 --> 00:01:11.160
a one-stop shop for vegan dog treats.

00:01:11.796 --> 00:01:13.579
You might decide to create a workflow

00:01:13.579 --> 00:01:15.823
to fulfill orders for specific dog treats.

00:01:16.527 --> 00:01:18.219
A new order kicks off the workflow.

00:01:18.759 --> 00:01:22.001
First, we check Cloud Firestore
to see if we have enough inventory.

00:01:22.508 --> 00:01:25.098
If we do, we update
the inventory in the database.

00:01:25.631 --> 00:01:28.071
Then, a Cloud Run app fulfills the request

00:01:28.071 --> 00:01:30.337
and calls a third-party email API

00:01:30.337 --> 00:01:32.474
to send the customer
an order confirmation.

00:01:33.357 --> 00:01:34.821
If there isn't inventory,

00:01:34.821 --> 00:01:36.539
the workflow can check once per day

00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:38.129
until the item is back in stock.

00:01:39.154 --> 00:01:42.360
So next time you're looking
to automate your application services,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.057
take a minute to look at Workflows.

00:01:44.519 --> 00:01:48.332
To learn more, check out
<i>cloud.google.com/workflows</i>

